sections.s2_technical:
`Stream.ofNullable` eliminates the null-check-then-stream pattern that was common before Java 9.

```java
// BEFORE Java 9: verbose null check
String symbol = getSymbolOrNull();
Stream<String> stream = symbol != null ? Stream.of(symbol) : Stream.empty();

// AFTER Java 9: one-liner
Stream<String> stream = Stream.ofNullable(getSymbolOrNull());
// null → Stream.empty(). "RELIANCE" → Stream.of("RELIANCE").
```

The most powerful use is inside `flatMap` to safely include nullable fields from multiple sources.

```java
// Collect all non-null optional fields from a trade:
List<String> allNotes = trades.stream()
    .flatMap(t -> Stream.ofNullable(t.getRiskNote()))  // null notes become empty → skipped
    .toList();
// Only non-null risk notes collected. No NullPointerException. No filter(Objects::nonNull).
```

`Stream.concat` merges two streams lazily. The resulting stream pulls from streamA first. When streamA is exhausted, it pulls from streamB. Neither stream is consumed until a terminal operation is invoked on the concatenated stream.

```java
// Merge two trade lists from different sources:
Stream<Trade> combined = Stream.concat(
    exchangeTrades.stream(),
    internalTrades.stream());
// No processing yet — lazy!

List<Trade> all = combined.filter(Trade::isSettled).toList();
// NOW both streams are consumed through the filter.

// Concat multiple streams (chain):
Stream<Trade> threeWay = Stream.concat(
    Stream.concat(exchangeTrades.stream(), internalTrades.stream()),
    manualTrades.stream());

// Or use Stream.of + flatMap for many streams:
Stream<Trade> multiWay = Stream.of(
    exchangeTrades.stream(),
    internalTrades.stream(),
    manualTrades.stream()
).flatMap(s -> s);  // Stream<Stream<Trade>> → Stream<Trade>
```

Important: concat creates a lazily concatenated stream. If both input streams are AutoCloseable (from Files.lines), closing the concatenated stream closes both inputs. But if only one needs closing, wrap in try-with-resources.

